Keep the virtual cursor continuous across cursor-mode transitions

glfwSetInputMode(GLFW_CURSOR, ...) re-reads the OS cursor position into
window->virtualCursorPos before the platform performs its enter/exit
warps, and without firing the cursor position callback. Applications
that track the cursor position from callbacks (ImGui among them) desync
by the drift accumulated during GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED and observe it
replayed as one giant delta on the next motion event — the long-standing
"cursor jump" of glfw/glfw#2523 / #1523.

Asymmetric fix:
- Entering GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED: no rebase at all. virtualCursorPos
  already equals the last position reported to the app, its absolute
  base is meaningless in disabled mode by definition, and the platform
  warp-to-center is already hidden from the delta stream. The virtual
  cursor is simply continuous: no event, no discontinuity.
- Leaving GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED: the platform warps the cursor back to
  the pre-capture position, a real teleport the app must learn about.
  Apply the platform mode change first, then report the settled
  position through _glfwInputCursorPos so the callback fires and
  consumers resync. Ordering matters: reporting before the platform
  warp still leaves the restore-warp discontinuity unreported.

Contract note: a cursor-pos callback may now arrive synchronously
inside glfwSetInputMode when leaving disabled mode; it reports the
restored cursor position and should be treated as a resync, not motion.
Verified with repro/cursor_jump_repro.c (repo usdtweak-jumpingcursorv2).

--- a/src/input.c	2026-07-01 21:59:35
+++ b/src/input.c	2026-07-01 22:25:44
@@ -607,12 +607,38 @@
             if (window->cursorMode == value)
                 return;
 
-            window->cursorMode = value;
+            {
+                const int oldMode = window->cursorMode;
+                window->cursorMode = value;
 
-            _glfw.platform.getCursorPos(window,
-                                        &window->virtualCursorPosX,
-                                        &window->virtualCursorPosY);
-            _glfw.platform.setCursorMode(window, value);
+                // usdtweak patch (glfw/glfw#2523): upstream re-reads the OS
+                // cursor position into virtualCursorPos here — before the
+                // platform performs its enter/exit warps, and without firing
+                // the cursor position callback — so apps tracking the
+                // position from callbacks (e.g. ImGui) desync and see the
+                // discontinuity replayed as one giant delta on the next
+                // motion event. Instead:
+                //  - Entering GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED: do nothing. The virtual
+                //    cursor continues from the last reported position (its
+                //    absolute base is meaningless by definition) and the
+                //    platform's warp-to-center is already hidden from the
+                //    delta stream. No rebase, no event, no discontinuity.
+                //  - Leaving GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED: the platform warps the
+                //    cursor back to the pre-capture position — a real
+                //    teleport. Apply the mode first, then report the settled
+                //    position through _glfwInputCursorPos so the callback
+                //    fires and consumers resync.
+                //  - Transitions not involving DISABLED don't move the
+                //    cursor; nothing to do.
+                _glfw.platform.setCursorMode(window, value);
+
+                if (value != GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED && oldMode == GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED)
+                {
+                    double xpos, ypos;
+                    _glfw.platform.getCursorPos(window, &xpos, &ypos);
+                    _glfwInputCursorPos(window, xpos, ypos);
+                }
+            }
             return;
         }
 
